4 0 {"title": "The F6 non-vacuity case has no positive twin for any of its rejections, and its read rejection does not show protection", "verdict": "confirmed", "severity": "major", "reasoning": "The brief, step 5, says: 'F7 et cas, avec un jumeau positif pour chaque rejet'. Every other pass-6 case in the module has an ok twin: na4 has reset reject and then ok, lb5 and vw3 each transfer reject and ok, sr0 has store reject and ok, se2 has endUse reject and ok, tx0 has transfer reject and the tmp twin ok. The F6 non-vacuity block (MM03.lean:9164-9190) has five rejections and none of them has an ok twin. `grep pv0|pv1` finds no other example. I reran pv.lean and pv2.lean. Their output reproduces the claims: in releaseEff pv0 1, the triple (exercisable, effective, init) for the read through 0 is (false, true, false). After the exit, the read is still rejected, because the triple is (canRead3=false, init=false, exercisable=true). canRead3 (MM03.lean:1378) is `ExercisableAt a .observe ∧ ∀ p ∈ ls, CanReadLeaf s a p`, so the read rejection has two causes. One part of the finding overstates: non-exercisability is one of the two causes, so it is wrong to say the read rejection 'says nothing' about protection. Still, the rejection does not tell protection apart from an uninitialised leaf, and in this setup no ok twin for the read exists. I checked that the proposed twins can be built (scratchpad/rev12/integrity_judge/twins.lean compiles with no output).", "evidence": "MM03.lean:9179-9181 (Write3Step/Read3Step/DeriveStep reject on releaseEff pv0 1) and 9188-9189 (Write3Step 1 and 0 reject on releaseEff pv1 2); none of them has an ok counterpart. `lake env lean rev12/integrity/pv.lean` prints ('read r1 0 [0]: exercisable, effective, init', false, true, false) and ('read after exit', false, false, true). /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/rev12/integrity_judge/twins.lean compiles. In it, pw0 := enterEff (deriveEff (write3Eff (enter1 [(none,none,0)]) [0]) 0 0 .modify) [1] []; Read3Step 1 [0] pw0 ok; Read3Step 0 [0] (releaseEff pw0 1) reject with I 0 = .init and ¬ExercisableAt 0 .observe; ExitStep ok and then Read3Step 0 [0] ok; ExitStep K0 (releaseEff pv1 2) ok and then Write3Step 1 [0] ok.", "fix": "Module, F6 non-vacuity section: (1) add ok twins for the access through the active parameter: `Write3Step 1 [0] pv0 (.ok _)` and `Write3Step 2 [0] pv1 (.ok _)`. (2) Add twins for the caller after the return:

3 1 {"title": "F6 non-vacuity cases (pv*) have no positive twin for any of their rejections", "verdict": "confirmed", "severity": "minor", "reasoning": "Step 5 of the brief (task_mm03_p6.md:38) requires 'F7 et cas, avec un jumeau positif pour chaque rejet', and line 47 lists the F6 case with 'paramètre libéré puis accès tenté → reject'. The pv block (MM03.lean:9173-9190) has five rejections: Write3Step 0 [0], Read3Step 0 [0] and DeriveStep 0 0 .modify on releaseEff pv0 1, and Write3Step 1 [0] and Write3Step 0 [0] on releaseEff pv1 2. Its only `.ok` steps are ReleaseStep and EnterStep, which are not the rejected accesses accepted in a changed situation. The pv docstring (9165-9172) names no twin. Every other pass-6 case does carry a twin, including ones the brief did not spell out: sr0 store root reject vs derived store ok (9127-9131); se2 endUse reject, release, then endUse ok (9145-9150); tx0 (9157-9162); vw3 (9108-9109). So the gap is specific to the pv block. Twins exist and compile, so nothing blocks adding them.", "evidence": "task_mm03_p6.md:38 and :47; MM03.lean:9173-9190 (the only .ok steps are ReleaseStep 1 pv0, EnterStep [2] [] and ReleaseStep 2 pv1). Lean check in /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/rev12/f356judge/j2.lean compiles with no output. It contains `Write3Step 1 [0] pv0 (.ok (write3Eff pv0 [0]))`, `Read3Step 1 [0] (write3Eff pv0 [0]) (.ok _)`, `ExitStep K0 (releaseEff pv0 1) (.ok (exitEff (releaseEff pv0 1)))`, `Write3Step 0 [0] pvx (.ok _)`, `Read3Step 0 [0] (write3Eff pvx [0]) (.ok _)` and `DeriveStep 0 0 .modify pvx (.ok _)` with pvx := exitEff (releaseEff pv0 1). For pv1, with pvy := exitEff (releaseEff pv1 2), it contains `ExitStep K0 (releaseEff pv1 2) (.ok pvy)`, `Write3Step 1 [0] pvy (.ok _)`, `ReleaseStep 1 pvy (.ok _)`, `ExitStep K0 (releaseEff pvy 1) (.ok _)` and `Write3Step 0 [0] (exitEff (releaseEff pvy 1)) (.ok _)`. One note: Read3Step 1 [0] pv0 and Read3Step 0 [0] pvx without a prior write are rejected because leaf 0 is not initialised (j.lean), so a read twin needs a write first.", "fix": "In formal/Ern/MM03.lean, Non-vacuity of F6 section, add these twins after line 9190 and name them in the pv docstring. They are expected `ok`: the callee writes and reads through the parameter before releasing it; after release and exit, the caller writes, reads and derives through root 0; in the nested case, after release and exit of frame 3, frame 2 writes through 1

2 1 {"title": "progress_cycle_whole_nodes docstring presents rejections as effects of the cycle, but two of them already occur before it; its permanence is unproven", "verdict": "partly", "severity": "minor", "reasoning": "Reproduced. The statement (MM03.lean:9216-9222) proves only one-step rejections in fa8, plus the exit facts. The rejections the docstring lists after the colon do not depend on the cycle. f7a.lean (compiles) gives `ResetAtStep K0 0 0 fa4 .reject` and `EndUseStep K0 2 fa7 .reject`, while `ReleaseStep 8 fa7` is ok. My own check, /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-guygouri-Devlab-ernlang-ern/c986905c-e900-4fd9-bebc-4a3ce7ebb180/scratchpad/rev12/f7judge/j1.lean, finds endUse 3 rejected even in fa5, before any store (`decide (EndUsePre K0 fa5 3) = false`). As a one-step fact, the rejection of endUse/reset in fa8 is no different from the one in fa7. Its only added content would be that it is permanent, and nothing proves that. My one-step probe in j1.lean finds no release accepted in fa8 and endUse accepted only for 1. That still leaves the other operations and longer sequences, so the question stays open. The finding overstates in one place: the docstring is not literally false. Read as statements about fa8, each listed rejection is proven, and \"The exit of frame 1 releases both\" does not say \"only\". What reproduces is the misleading causal colon and the unproven deadlock reading, which the brief's rule covers: \"une séquence non trouvée est un résultat ouvert\".", "evidence": "MM03.lean:9200-9202: \"After it, 7 and 8 are each exercisable only through the other: neither is exercisable, neither can be released, the node values cannot end, the arena cannot be reset.\" f7a.lean: `ResetAtStep K0 0 0 fa4 .reject`, `EndUseStep K0 2 fa7 .reject`, `ReleaseStep 8 fa7 (.ok _)`. j1.lean output: release candidates in fa8 = [], endUse candidates in fa8 = [1], `decide (EndUsePre K0 fa5 3) = false`.", "fix": "Reword the docstring at MM03.lean:9200-9202: \"In `fa8`, neither 7 nor 8 is exercisable, and `release` of 7 or 8, `endUse` of 2 or 3 and `reset` are rejected. The `endUse` and `reset` rejections already hold before the second store (reset from `fa4`), where 8 could still be released first. Whether frame 1 can free 7 or 8 before its exit is open. The exit releases both.\" Repeat the open question in report section (p)."}
